WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.325
Welcome to Session three.

2
00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:12.325
This is the session about biological databases, and it is the one that decides whether everything else you do in this course can be repeated by anybody, including you.

3
00:00:12.325 --> 00:00:16.275
That sounds like an overstatement, so let me justify it.

4
00:00:16.275 --> 00:00:20.725
Every session from here on starts the same way: you fetch something.

5
00:00:20.725 --> 00:00:23.800
A sequence to align in Session four.

6
00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.350
A family to build a profile from in Session five.

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:30.400
A structure to compare in Session six.

8
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.775
A training set in Sessions seven to twelve.

9
00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:45.200
If you cannot say precisely what you fetched, then your alignment, your profile and your model are all unrepeatable — not wrong, necessarily, but impossible to check, which in practice is worse.

10
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.850
So this session has three jobs.

11
00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:52.300
The first is to show how the main databases relate to one another.

12
00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:58.325
Not a tour of websites: a map of which resource answers which question, and how you move between them.

13
00:00:58.325 --> 00:01:04.325
The second is to ask, of every claim in every record: who says so, and on what evidence?

14
00:01:04.325 --> 00:01:14.575
That question sounds pedantic until the first time you build an analysis on an annotation that turns out to have been generated by a program with no one checking it.

15
00:01:14.575 --> 00:01:22.050
The third is to build a habit — writing down what you used, precisely enough that somebody else gets exactly the same thing.

16
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:26.125
The protein we follow is human beta-globin, HBB.

17
00:01:26.125 --> 00:01:36.200
It is a good thread because it is small, it is extremely well studied, it has hundreds of experimental structures, and it causes a disease everybody has heard of.

18
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:44.450
Almost every database in this lecture has something to say about it, which means we can see the same molecule from a dozen different angles.

19
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.575
Here is the shape of the lecture.

20
00:01:47.575 --> 00:01:56.200
Five questions about one protein, and each database turns up as the answer to one of them, rather than as an item on a list to memorise.

21
00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.100
That framing is deliberate, and I want to explain why.

22
00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.675
There are thousands of biological databases.

23
00:02:03.675 --> 00:02:10.375
New ones appear every month, old ones are absorbed or abandoned, and a list of names has a short shelf life.

24
00:02:10.375 --> 00:02:23.225
What does not go out of date is knowing which *kind* of question each resource answers — so that when you have a question, you know what sort of place to look, even if the particular site has changed since you were taught about it.

25
00:02:23.225 --> 00:02:26.350
The five questions run roughly like this.

26
00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.450
What is the sequence?

27
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.275
Where does the gene sit in the genome?

28
00:02:31.275 --> 00:02:33.500
What does the protein do?

29
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.550
What does it look like in three dimensions?

30
00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.050
And what does it interact with?

31
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:43.650
Each one lands you somewhere different, and the records point at each other.

32
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.700
A small story, because it is in the notes and it makes the point better than I can.

33
00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:53.075
Why do databases trust accession numbers more than gene names?

34
00:02:53.075 --> 00:02:55.550
Because gene names change.

35
00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.450
There was once a gene called Pokemon.

36
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:06.125
In two thousand and five the owners of the Pokémon franchise objected, and it was renamed Z b t b seven.

37
00:03:06.125 --> 00:03:15.025
Every paper published before that uses one name, every paper after uses the other, and anybody searching by name gets half the literature.

38
00:03:15.025 --> 00:03:17.400
The accession never moved.

39
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.475
Block one.

40
00:03:19.475 --> 00:03:24.150
The databases themselves, and the identifiers that hold them together.

41
00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:31.650
The first thing to understand is that the nucleotide sequence archives are not three competing databases.

42
00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.775
They are one archive with three front doors.

43
00:03:34.775 --> 00:03:56.050
The International Nucleotide Sequence Database Collaboration — INSDC — is GenBank, run by the National Center for Biotechnology Information in Bethesda, Maryland; the European Nucleotide Archive, run by EMBL-EBI at Hinxton near Cambridge; and the DNA Data Bank of Japan, run by the National Institute of Genetics in Mishima.

44
00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.675
They exchange records daily.

45
00:03:58.675 --> 00:04:04.375
A sequence submitted to any one of them can be found through all three, under the same accession.

46
00:04:04.375 --> 00:04:06.600
The pages look different.

47
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:17.125
The flat-file formats differ in their details — GenBank and ENA lay out a record differently, and if you have only ever seen one you will find the other briefly disorienting.

48
00:04:17.125 --> 00:04:22.750
What does not differ is the accession, and what does not differ is the annotation standard.

49
00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:25.450
Two practical consequences.

50
00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:32.525
First, you submit to one partner, not to three; sending the same data to all of them is not thoroughness, it is a mess.

51
00:04:32.525 --> 00:04:37.650
Second, when a colleague hands you an accession, you can look it up wherever you prefer.

52
00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.475
And one historical consequence worth appreciating.

53
00:04:41.475 --> 00:04:46.125
Deposition has been required by most journals since the nineteen-nineties.

54
00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:51.825
That means a sequence you look up today reflects essentially everything that has been published.

55
00:04:51.825 --> 00:04:57.025
The archive is not a sample of the literature; it is very close to all of it.

56
00:04:57.025 --> 00:05:05.350
There are few fields where that is true, and it is the reason sequence bioinformatics got started earlier than most computational biology.

57
00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:10.525
One more thing about the three partners, because it explains something you will notice.

58
00:05:10.525 --> 00:05:14.425
They do not hold identical *views* of the data.

59
00:05:14.425 --> 00:05:24.925
ENA, for instance, holds both the read data and the assembled sequences under one roof, whereas at NCBI those live in separate resources with separate names.

60
00:05:24.925 --> 00:05:31.150
So the same underlying record is reachable from all three, but the surrounding furniture differs.

61
00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:37.675
That is why the boxes on this slide should not be read as four databases that each partner has a copy of.

62
00:05:37.675 --> 00:05:46.450
They are kinds of record — the study, the sample, the reads, the sequence — and the partners organise them differently while agreeing on what they contain.

63
00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:52.075
Two things that do not come up anywhere else in this lecture, and both of them matter.

64
00:05:52.075 --> 00:05:57.125
The first is that a sequence record is the end of a chain, not the whole of it.

65
00:05:57.125 --> 00:05:59.150
Follow it backwards.

66
00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:03.300
The study is a BioProject record — who did this, and why.

67
00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:09.450
The material that was sequenced is a BioSample — which organism, which tissue, which individual.

68
00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:15.675
The raw reads are a run in the Sequence Read Archive, or its European or Japanese equivalents.

69
00:06:15.675 --> 00:06:21.600
And only then, at the end, comes the assembled, annotated sequence that lands in GenBank.

70
00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:24.300
A paper cites the last of those.

71
00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.675
The evidence for it is one box to the left.

72
00:06:27.675 --> 00:06:30.600
That distinction has a practical use.

73
00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:41.850
If somebody reports a variant and you want to know whether it is real — rather than an artefact of how the reads were filtered, or trimmed, or aligned — the consensus sequence cannot tell you.

74
00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.675
The reads can.

75
00:06:43.675 --> 00:06:49.975
They are deposited precisely so that somebody can go back and check, and almost nobody ever does.

76
00:06:49.975 --> 00:06:52.925
The second thing is that archives move.

77
00:06:52.925 --> 00:06:57.175
The NCBI Trace Archive ran from two thousand and one.

78
00:06:57.175 --> 00:07:07.975
It held chromatograms from the big genome projects — the raw instrument traces, those coloured peaks, together with the sequences read off them, the quality scores and the metadata.

79
00:07:07.975 --> 00:07:16.075
When high-throughput sequencing arrived with far larger datasets, NCBI built the Sequence Read Archive in two thousand and eight.

80
00:07:16.075 --> 00:07:19.550
The two ran side by side for over a decade.

81
00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:26.375
Then, in June twenty twenty-two, the Trace Archive was retired and its contents moved into SRA.

82
00:07:26.375 --> 00:07:29.650
Japan's equivalent closed the following year.

83
00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:34.875
Europe had taken a third route, folding everything into ENA back in twenty ten.

84
00:07:34.875 --> 00:07:38.300
Nothing was lost in any of those three cases.

85
00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.950
But every link anybody had published stopped working.

86
00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:48.925
That is the real argument for understanding how these resources relate, rather than memorising their addresses.

87
00:07:48.925 --> 00:07:51.400
The data usually survive.

88
00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:53.475
The address does not.

89
00:07:54.075 --> 00:07:59.925
Here is a question that used to have a different answer: should you keep your own copy of a database?

90
00:07:59.925 --> 00:08:02.775
Twenty years ago, yes, routinely.

91
00:08:02.775 --> 00:08:08.700
You downloaded a release, you indexed it, you searched it locally, and you knew exactly what you had.

92
00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.050
Now, consider the size.

93
00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:19.950
TrEMBL — the unreviewed section of UniProt, which we come back to in block two — holds around a hundred and forty-nine million entries.

94
00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:22.250
You can still download it.

95
00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.800
People do.

96
00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:36.150
But a private copy goes stale the moment you make it, and keeping it current means downloads, updates, indexes, backups and the software to search them, maintained by somebody whose actual job is something else.

97
00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.800
So the databases have become shared infrastructure.

98
00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:44.675
Public institutions maintain them, and you query them rather than owning them.

99
00:08:44.675 --> 00:08:49.050
There is one important exception, and it is not a contradiction.

100
00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:56.250
A *fixed snapshot*, taken deliberately for a specific analysis and kept, is not staleness.

101
00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.750
It is reproducibility.

102
00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:08.175
If your paper says "searched against UniProt release twenty twenty-six underscore oh three", that release still exists and somebody can repeat your search.

103
00:09:08.175 --> 00:09:11.775
If it says "searched against UniProt", they cannot.

104
00:09:11.775 --> 00:09:17.625
There is a second, less obvious cost to a private copy: you stop noticing corrections.

105
00:09:17.625 --> 00:09:20.325
Databases do not only grow.

106
00:09:20.325 --> 00:09:31.650
Entries are revised, merged, and occasionally withdrawn — a sequence turns out to be contaminated, or a gene model is corrected, or two records turn out to describe the same thing.

107
00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:35.750
If you query the live resource, you get the correction for free.

108
00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:42.475
If you search a copy you took in March, you are still working with whatever was wrong in March, and nothing will tell you.

109
00:09:42.475 --> 00:09:50.600
So the rule of thumb is: query the live service for exploration, and pin a specific release for the analysis you are going to publish.

110
00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:57.025
Those are different activities with different requirements, and it is worth being conscious of which one you are doing.

111
00:09:57.625 --> 00:10:02.550
Here are seven identifiers, all referring to one gene and its products.

112
00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:08.575
The sentence to take from this slide is: an accession is unique only within its own database.

113
00:10:08.575 --> 00:10:17.125
The same string can mean different things in different resources, and the same molecule has a different identifier in every resource that holds it.

114
00:10:17.125 --> 00:10:19.700
There is no universal name.

115
00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:25.275
Two of these are worth distinguishing carefully, because they look equally official and are not.

116
00:10:25.275 --> 00:10:29.400
HBB underscore HUMAN is an *entry name*.

117
00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:36.450
It is readable, it is convenient, and it can change — UniProt reserves the right to rename entries, and does.

118
00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:40.525
P six eight eight seven one is the *accession*.

119
00:10:40.525 --> 00:10:43.875
It is stable, and it is the thing to write down.

120
00:10:43.875 --> 00:10:46.850
That is a general pattern, by the way.

121
00:10:46.850 --> 00:10:53.750
Where a database offers both a readable label and an opaque identifier, the opaque one is the stable one.

122
00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:59.250
The readability is bought by making it describe something, and descriptions get revised.

123
00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:01.650
One more thing on this slide.

124
00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.450
HBB appears in hundreds of PDB entries.

125
00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:09.275
Two H H B is one of them, not *the* one.

126
00:11:09.275 --> 00:11:14.100
If you say "the structure of haemoglobin", you have not specified anything.

127
00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.975
A practical note on reading identifiers aloud, because you will have to.

128
00:11:18.975 --> 00:11:27.650
Accessions are case sensitive, and the distinction between letter O and digit zero, or letter l and digit one, has caused real trouble.

129
00:11:27.650 --> 00:11:32.375
When you copy an accession, copy it — do not retype it from a screenshot.

130
00:11:32.375 --> 00:11:38.950
And when a colleague reads one to you over a coffee, write it down and check it against the record before you use it.

131
00:11:38.950 --> 00:11:40.950
That sounds trivial.

132
00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:49.250
It is the same category of advice as checking the organism, and it fails in the same way: silently, with a result that looks fine.

133
00:11:49.850 --> 00:11:56.450
Now look closely at the structure of a single accession, because it carries more information than people realise.

134
00:11:56.450 --> 00:12:05.450
N M underscore zero zero zero five one eight is a *record*: the RefSeq messenger RNA for beta-globin.

135
00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:07.425
It names the thing.

136
00:12:07.425 --> 00:12:15.775
N M underscore zero zero zero five one eight point five is one exact *sequence* — version five of that record.

137
00:12:15.775 --> 00:12:19.750
The version number increments whenever the sequence changes.

138
00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:24.625
Annotation can be edited without a version bump; the sequence cannot.

139
00:12:24.625 --> 00:12:31.725
So quoting the record without the version says: "this gene's messenger RNA, whatever it happens to be today".

140
00:12:31.725 --> 00:12:35.825
Quoting it with the version says: "these exact bases".

141
00:12:35.825 --> 00:12:38.425
Only the second can be checked.

142
00:12:38.425 --> 00:12:48.500
This is the single most common reproducibility mistake in sequence bioinformatics, and it costs literally nothing to avoid — it is five extra characters.

143
00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:58.450
When somebody cannot reproduce an analysis from six years ago, this is very often why: the record was updated, and the paper did not say which version it used.

144
00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:03.975
This is the head of that record, as NCBI actually returns it.

145
00:13:03.975 --> 00:13:15.200
It is the first time in this course that you are looking at a real record rather than a diagram of one, and I want to spend a moment on it because the habit it teaches is the one the lab will ask for.

146
00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:17.875
Four fields do most of the work.

147
00:13:17.875 --> 00:13:21.275
LOCUS names the record and gives its length.

148
00:13:21.275 --> 00:13:25.400
If the length is not what you expect, stop — you have the wrong thing.

149
00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:28.400
VERSION fixes the exact sequence.

150
00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.550
This is the field you copy into your notes.

151
00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:35.125
KEYWORDS, on this record, contains MANE Select.

152
00:13:35.125 --> 00:13:44.600
That is a flag we will come back to in a few slides, and it means NCBI and EMBL-EBI have agreed this is the representative transcript for this gene.

153
00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:47.650
SOURCE says which organism it came from.

154
00:13:47.650 --> 00:13:54.550
More analyses have been wrecked by a mouse sequence in a human dataset than by any subtle algorithmic error.

155
00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:58.125
Read those four, in that order, before you use anything.

156
00:13:58.125 --> 00:14:00.350
It takes ten seconds.

157
00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:09.150
One detail about the slide itself, which matters for a different reason: this figure is fetched from NCBI by a script, not screenshotted.

158
00:14:09.150 --> 00:14:13.050
Re-running the script refreshes it when the record changes.

159
00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:17.075
A screenshot would quietly become a historical document.

160
00:14:17.675 --> 00:14:20.750
Databases follow the central dogma.

161
00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:26.750
DNA, RNA and protein each get their own records, and the records point at each other.

162
00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:29.550
Three layers are worth distinguishing.

163
00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:36.725
The raw submitted sequences sit in INSDC — everything anybody deposited, redundancy and all.

164
00:14:36.725 --> 00:14:45.925
RefSeq and Ensembl sit on top of that and give an annotated, non-redundant view: one chosen representative rather than every submission.

165
00:14:45.925 --> 00:14:57.500
And genome browsers such as UCSC and Ensembl present the gene in its chromosomal context, which is the view you want when the question is about regulation rather than about sequence.

166
00:14:57.500 --> 00:15:02.600
The cross-references are what make this navigable, and they are the feature to lean on.

167
00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:09.350
The UniProt entry for beta-globin lists both the messenger RNA accession and the protein accession.

168
00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:18.750
So from one record you can reach the others directly, without going back to a search box and typing a name — which, as we will see, is where mistakes come from.

169
00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:27.175
A question that comes up here every year: if RefSeq and Ensembl both give an annotated non-redundant view, why are there two of them?

170
00:15:27.175 --> 00:15:34.950
Historically, because they were built by different institutions with different annotation pipelines and different editorial judgements.

171
00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:40.200
They disagree, at the margins, about where genes start and which transcripts are real.

172
00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:44.525
For most genes the disagreement is small; for some it is not.

173
00:15:44.525 --> 00:15:53.275
MANE is the response to that — a jointly agreed representative transcript, identical in both, which is what the next few slides are about.

174
00:15:53.275 --> 00:16:02.350
It does not merge the two annotation sets, but it does mean that for a human protein-coding gene there is now one transcript both sides point at.

175
00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:05.950
This is not four kinds of thing in a row.

176
00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:15.150
It is the same DNA at four scales, and that distinction is worth making explicit because the diagram can be misread as a hierarchy of objects.

177
00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:21.075
A genome assembly is a reconstruction of the whole sequence of a genome, built from fragments.

178
00:16:21.075 --> 00:16:26.225
A chromosome is one piece of that assembly, with numbered positions along it.

179
00:16:26.225 --> 00:16:29.325
A region is a window on a chromosome.

180
00:16:29.325 --> 00:16:32.475
A gene is a feature inside the window.

181
00:16:32.475 --> 00:16:38.375
What ties them together is the coordinate system: the assembly supplies it, all the way down.

182
00:16:38.375 --> 00:16:46.350
Chromosome eleven position five million and something only means anything relative to a particular assembly of the human genome.

183
00:16:46.350 --> 00:16:54.800
Which is why the nesting matters, and it is the subject of the next slide but one: a coordinate without its assembly is not a coordinate.

184
00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.550
It is a number.

185
00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:01.625
One gene symbol, several transcripts, and they are not interchangeable.

186
00:17:01.625 --> 00:17:06.250
HBB dash two zero one is the MANE Select transcript.

187
00:17:06.250 --> 00:17:19.675
MANE stands for Matched Annotation from NCBI and EMBL-EBI, and it is an agreement between the two about which transcript represents a gene — the same sequence with the same coordinates in both RefSeq and Ensembl.

188
00:17:19.675 --> 00:17:27.450
For a human protein-coding gene, if you need one transcript and have no reason to prefer another, this is the one to use.

189
00:17:27.450 --> 00:17:32.375
It is the same sequence as the RefSeq record we read a moment ago.

190
00:17:32.375 --> 00:17:35.875
The other three on this slide differ in real ways.

191
00:17:35.875 --> 00:17:40.950
One starts transcription at a different site, so it has a different first exon.

192
00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:43.625
One reaches further upstream.

193
00:17:43.625 --> 00:17:47.100
One retains an intron that the others splice out.

194
00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:52.525
Filled boxes are coding sequence; open boxes are untranslated regions.

195
00:17:52.525 --> 00:17:56.175
Ensembl lists nineteen transcripts for this gene.

196
00:17:56.175 --> 00:18:00.200
So "I used HBB" does not specify a sequence.

197
00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:05.525
It specifies a gene symbol, from which nineteen different sequences could follow.

198
00:18:05.525 --> 00:18:14.650
A report names the transcript, not the gene — and that is not pedantry, because for some genes the transcripts encode genuinely different proteins.

199
00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:23.375
RefSeq encodes two separate things in the prefix of an accession, and once you know them you can read a great deal before opening anything.

200
00:18:23.375 --> 00:18:26.625
The letter pair tells you the biological level.

201
00:18:26.625 --> 00:18:29.950
N M and X M are messenger RNA.

202
00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:32.775
N P and X P are protein.

203
00:18:32.775 --> 00:18:35.550
N C and N G are genomic.

204
00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:38.450
The first letter tells you the provenance.

205
00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:44.750
X means predicted — a program inferred this record from a genome, and no human looked at it.

206
00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:49.900
N means not predicted, and many N records have been curated by hand.

207
00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:55.425
Put those together and X P tells you "predicted protein" before you have clicked anything.

208
00:18:55.425 --> 00:19:01.150
If your analysis depends on that sequence being right, that is a flag worth noticing.

209
00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:04.675
One point of vocabulary that confuses people.

210
00:19:04.675 --> 00:19:10.925
RefSeq is built *from* INSDC data, but it is not itself part of INSDC.

211
00:19:10.925 --> 00:19:15.325
It is a curated, non-redundant layer on top of the archive.

212
00:19:15.325 --> 00:19:18.850
The archive keeps everything; RefSeq chooses.

213
00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:24.175
And here is one of those predicted records, fetched live in the notebook.

214
00:19:24.175 --> 00:19:27.125
The important thing is that it tells you.

215
00:19:27.125 --> 00:19:32.550
You do not have to infer it from the prefix, or guess from the quality of the annotation.

216
00:19:32.550 --> 00:19:38.025
The record carries its own provenance, in a field that says MODEL REFSEQ.

217
00:19:38.025 --> 00:19:47.400
That is the theme of block two in miniature: good databases tell you where their information came from, and the skill is learning to read that rather than ignoring it.

218
00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:52.775
It is worth saying what "predicted" actually means here, because it is not guesswork.

219
00:19:52.775 --> 00:20:02.150
A gene-prediction program has looked at genomic DNA, found an open reading frame with plausible splice sites, and written down the protein it would produce.

220
00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.750
For a well-studied organism that is usually right.

221
00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:17.625
For a newly sequenced genome, with few related sequences to compare against, it can be wrong about where the gene starts, about which exons are included, or about whether there is a gene there at all.

222
00:20:17.625 --> 00:20:21.025
None of which makes predicted records useless.

223
00:20:21.025 --> 00:20:25.875
Most of what we know about most genomes is predicted, and it has to be.

224
00:20:25.875 --> 00:20:37.700
It makes them records whose provenance you should carry forward: if your result depends on this sequence being exactly right, say in your methods that it was a predicted record, so that a reader can weigh it.

225
00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:44.325
A coordinate is a position within a particular assembly, and the human genome has had several.

226
00:20:44.325 --> 00:20:53.950
GRCh37 — also called hg19 — and GRCh38, also called hg38, are both still in active use today.

227
00:20:53.950 --> 00:21:01.175
Older clinical datasets are often on GRCh37; most new work is on GRCh38.

228
00:21:01.175 --> 00:21:04.700
And the same gene has different positions in the two.

229
00:21:04.700 --> 00:21:07.300
Not slightly different: different.

230
00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:11.450
So a position quoted without its build is not a position.

231
00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:14.750
If you report coordinates, report the build.

232
00:21:14.750 --> 00:21:18.375
If somebody gives you coordinates without one, ask.

233
00:21:18.375 --> 00:21:21.350
This particular figure is schematic.

234
00:21:21.350 --> 00:21:28.925
The lab shows you the real coordinates of a gene in both builds, and the size of the shift surprises most people the first time.

235
00:21:28.925 --> 00:21:31.875
Why does the genome get rebuilt at all?

236
00:21:31.875 --> 00:21:36.625
Because an assembly is a reconstruction, and reconstructions improve.

237
00:21:36.625 --> 00:21:45.375
Gaps get filled as sequencing technology reaches regions it could not read before — long repeats, centromeres, segmental duplications.

238
00:21:45.375 --> 00:21:47.525
Errors get corrected.

239
00:21:47.525 --> 00:21:50.725
Occasionally a whole region is revised.

240
00:21:50.725 --> 00:22:02.850
GRCh38 also introduced alternative loci: regions where human populations differ so much that one reference sequence cannot represent them, so the assembly carries several.

241
00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:11.200
That is biologically honest and computationally awkward, and it is a good illustration of why "the human genome" is a simplification.

242
00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.175
The practical upshot is unchanged.

243
00:22:14.175 --> 00:22:16.075
Record the build.

244
00:22:16.075 --> 00:22:28.650
A variant at a position in GRCh37 is a different variant at the same number in GRCh38, and clinical databases have had to do a great deal of careful work to migrate between them.

245
00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:44.975
And here is what it looks like when it is done properly: chromosome eleven, from position five million two hundred and twenty-five thousand four hundred and sixty-four to five million two hundred and twenty-nine thousand three hundred and ninety-five, on GRCh38.

246
00:22:44.975 --> 00:22:48.275
Several things are worth pointing out in this view.

247
00:22:48.275 --> 00:22:51.575
The ruler across the top gives the coordinates.

248
00:22:51.575 --> 00:22:58.325
Every annotated transcript in GENCODE is drawn, which is how you see the nineteen we mentioned.

249
00:22:58.325 --> 00:23:04.225
And the MANE Select row is marked, so you can find the representative transcript at a glance.

250
00:23:04.225 --> 00:23:08.225
But the thing I actually want you to notice is the title bar.

251
00:23:08.225 --> 00:23:15.650
It contains the region and the assembly — which is exactly, and completely, what a retrieval log needs to record.

252
00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:21.325
If you copy that one line into your notes, somebody else can return to the identical view.

253
00:23:21.325 --> 00:23:25.550
The lab asks you to produce one of these for beta-globin yourselves.

254
00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:27.750
Two habits.

255
00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:31.475
If you take nothing else from this lecture, take these two.

256
00:23:31.475 --> 00:23:34.700
The first is about moving between records.

257
00:23:34.700 --> 00:23:41.575
When you need to get from one record to a related one, follow a cross-reference rather than searching again by name.

258
00:23:41.575 --> 00:23:52.475
A fresh search by name can land you on the wrong gene, the wrong organism, or a paralogue with a similar name — and nothing will warn you, because the result looks perfectly reasonable.

259
00:23:52.475 --> 00:23:57.375
A cross-reference cannot do that; it points at one specific record.

260
00:23:57.375 --> 00:24:05.000
If you have no cross-reference to follow, then use a stable identifier, or the approved symbol together with the organism.

261
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:09.550
And when you arrive, check two things: the organism, and the sequence.

262
00:24:09.550 --> 00:24:12.650
Those two checks catch almost everything.

263
00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:15.600
The second habit is about reporting.

264
00:24:15.600 --> 00:24:25.475
When you report a sequence, report accession point version — N M zero zero zero five one eight point five, not N M zero zero zero five one eight.

265
00:24:25.475 --> 00:24:29.100
When you report coordinates, report the build with them.

266
00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:31.700
Neither habit costs anything.

267
00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:36.850
Both are the difference between an analysis somebody can repeat and one they cannot.

